\details{
The module draws a Manhattan plot of GWAS summary statistics: each
variant's -log10(p) against its position along the genome, chromosomes laid
end to end in alternating colours, with genome-wide and suggestive
significance lines. It wraps \code{\link[VizModules:dittoViz_scatterPlotInputsUI]{VizModules::dittoViz_scatterPlotInputsUI()}}, so
point labelling (\code{annotate.by} / \code{highlight.points}), hover data, colours and
the other scatter controls all apply; those inputs are documented there.

Columns are detected from the usual names (qqman, PLINK, REGENIE, GWAS
Catalog harmonised): e.g. \code{CHR} / \code{chromosome}, \code{BP} / \code{POS} /
\code{base_pair_location}, \code{P} / \code{p_value}, \code{SNP} / \code{rsid} / \code{variant_id}. Large
summary statistics are thinned by default: every variant below \code{thin.p} is
kept, plus a reproducible \code{thin.fraction} of the rest; the source-data
download describes the plotted (thinned) variants.
}
\section{Plot parameters and defaults}{

The following parameters can be accessed via UI inputs and/or the \code{defaults} argument:
\itemize{
\item \code{chr.col}, \code{bp.col}, \code{p.col}, \code{snp.col} - Chromosome, position, p-value and
variant columns (default: detected)
\item \code{sig.threshold} - Genome-wide significance line (default: 5e-8)
\item \code{suggestive.threshold} - Suggestive line (default: 1e-5)
\item \code{thin} - Thin the non-significant variants (default: TRUE)
\item \code{thin.p} - Variants below this p-value are always kept (default: 0.01)
\item \code{thin.fraction} - Fraction of the other variants kept (default: 0.25)
